{
  "about": [
    "How every SCE engine spells a finite 64-bit float as text - in JSON, in",
    "`_event.data`, and as the text of an untyped `<param>` - written out by",
    "hand from ECMAScript's own answer (Node 22, `String(x)`).",
    "",
    "One spelling: ECMA-262 Number::toString, radix 10. The digits are the",
    "fewest that read back as the same double (the one nearest the value when",
    "several fewest-digit candidates read back), written in decimal notation",
    "when 1e-6 <= |x| < 1e21 and as `d[.ddd]e[+-]n` otherwise; a whole value",
    "has no fraction (`5`, never `5.0`) and negative zero is `0`. SCXML's data",
    "model is ECMAScript (W3C SCXML Appendix B.1), so this is what a number",
    "means as text, and a script engine's own `JSON.stringify` already writes",
    "it - the engines that do not run one write the same bytes.",
    "",
    "Why a shared table: measured 2026-10-04, the C++ writer printed 0.1 as",
    "`0.10000000000000001` (`%.17g`), the Kotlin writer printed 1e21 as",
    "`1.0E21` and a whole value as `5.0`, and the Rust and Go writers printed",
    "1e21 as twenty-two digits and 1e-7 as `0.0000001`, each correct for its",
    "language and none for the wire. The same machine sent a different text",
    "on each engine, and nothing pinned any of them.",
    "",
    "Each case gives `bits`, the IEEE 754 binary64 pattern as sixteen hex",
    "digits (so no reader's number parser stands between the table and the",
    "value), and `text`, the exact spelling the writer must produce. A value",
    "that is not finite has no row: a JSON writer gives it `null` and the",
    "untyped wire text gives `NaN`, `Infinity` or `-Infinity`, as ECMAScript",
    "does, and a typed `sce-static` real cannot hold one. The rule is stated",
    "once, in ARCHITECTURE.md, 'JSON Number Text', which lists the readers."
  ],
